it's been exactly one hundred and five  years since the fateful events that led  to the sinking of the Titanic  1,500 people were killed when the luxury  ocean liner struck an iceberg and sank  more than two miles to the bottom of the  North Atlantic for more than a century  many of the belongings of the 2,200  passengers and crew have sat on the  ocean floor but this week the public got  its first look at this a gold locket  owned by first class passenger Virginia  Clark it was recovered from the wreckage  in 1994 and put on display this week at  Titanic the artifact exhibition at the  Luxor Casino in Las Vegas the locket was  found in a bag along with a pair of  cufflinks and some poker chips which  helped researchers connect the dots on  who the bag belonged to we know that  when Virginia felt the ship hit the  iceberg she got dressed went to find her  husband who was playing poker in the  smoking lounge he left the game came and  helped her get ready and then put her on  a lifeboat movie fans may see a tragic  similarity between Clark's locket and  the necklace at the heart of the  blockbuster film Titanic like the movies  heroine Virginia Clark survived the  disaster her husband did not  if we can bring some more information to  that person what kind of person they  were what they enjoyed doing why they  were on the ship  it just helps us bring the story of  Titanic into the present there's a lot  of Titanic frenzy right now some company  is trying to 3d image the entire wreck  because in 20 years they think it may  all be mail disappear may melt into the  sea yeah so you're a bit of a Titanic  buff physics I think you can call me a  Titanic aficionado Anthony I think I  qualify things we're learning
 

